  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nSurface high pressure will continue to offer seasonably warm and dry\nconditions through the early part of Saturday. However, we continue \nto monitor trends with Hurricane Isaias as the remnants from this \nstorm will track up into the Ohio Valley late Saturday, with \ncontinued impacts through Sunday.\n\nCloud cover will increase on Saturday with temperatures continuing to\ntrend seasonably warm. Winds will gradually increase throughout the\nafternoon as the system approaches. During the daytime hours, wind \ngusts will generally peak around 20-25 MPH out of the southeast. \nGusts are expected to increase to around 25-35 MPH Saturday evening \nand continue into the early morning hours on Sunday. These winds \nshould begin to subside a bit by daybreak Sunday, but could still \nobserve gusts up to 20-25 MPH through the morning hours before they \nsubside during the afternoon. Of course, cannot rule out some \nisolated stronger gusts with any embedded storms, but that potential \nappears to be limited.\n\nAs of now, it seems like precipitation will hold off through the \nmajority of the daytime hours Saturday, but we may begin to see the \nonset of rain near/south of the Ohio River in the early evening \nhours. Coverage in thunder will increase overnight as instability\nbuilds into the region, but not particularly expecting widespread\nthunderstorms. Coverage in the rain will become more scattered on\nSunday, with showers generally more favored across portions of \nsouthern OH/northern KY. As the system gets pulled to the northeast, \nrain chances will gradually diminish from west to east throughout the\nday Sunday.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nModels display a meridional flow pattern across the CONUS next week,\nwhich could result in some fluctuating temperatures and precip \nchances across our fa. A weak low pressure system and associated \ncold front will move through the region on Tuesday. As of now, precip\nchances remain pretty limited, with the highest probs in central \nOhio near 30%. The cold front will at least help usher in relatively\ncooler air for Wednesday, which will nudge temperatures back towards \nseasonal normals.",
